1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,178
Welcome to lesson sixteen: Helm and Kustomize.

2
00:00:04,278 --> 00:00:14,352
You'll stop copying YAML between environments. Helm packages applications with parameters and versioned releases, and Kustomize layers environment differences over plain YAML.

3
00:00:15,702 --> 00:00:19,712
A real app has many objects, running in several environments.

4
00:00:19,812 --> 00:00:25,758
Copying files per environment means they drift apart. Someone fixes a probe in dev and forgets production.

5
00:00:25,858 --> 00:00:33,228
Helm solves this with templates and values. Kustomize solves it with a plain YAML base and small patches for each environment.

6
00:00:34,578 --> 00:00:36,396
Helm has a few concepts.

7
00:00:36,496 --> 00:00:47,034
A chart is a package of templates and defaults. Values are your settings. A release is one installed copy, with a name, and each install, upgrade or rollback creates a new revision.

8
00:00:47,134 --> 00:00:54,456
Helm three and later have no server component. Release history is stored as Secrets in the cluster, which is what makes rollback work.

9
00:00:55,806 --> 00:01:01,768
To use a chart, add its repository, look at its values, and install it. Always pin the version.

10
00:01:01,868 --> 00:01:07,462
helm upgrade dash dash install is idempotent, so it's what CI pipelines usually run.

11
00:01:07,562 --> 00:01:15,524
history lists revisions, and rollback goes back to one, creating a new revision. helm template renders the YAML without installing anything.

12
00:01:15,624 --> 00:01:20,738
Values from files override the chart's defaults, and dash dash set overrides everything.

13
00:01:22,088 --> 00:01:24,642
helm create gives you a chart skeleton.

14
00:01:24,742 --> 00:01:30,432
Chart dot yaml holds the chart's own version, which you bump on every change, and the app version.

15
00:01:30,532 --> 00:01:39,486
A values schema validates input early. And CRDs in the crds folder are installed only once. Helm never upgrades or deletes them.

16
00:01:40,836 --> 00:01:43,214
Templates use Go's template language.

17
00:01:43,314 --> 00:01:52,156
default gives a fallback, required fails with a clear message, and quote wraps values in quotes. toYaml with nindent inserts a block at the right indentation.

18
00:01:52,256 --> 00:01:57,306
Indentation is the most common chart bug, so always check with helm template.

19
00:01:57,406 --> 00:02:03,640
And the checksum annotation changes whenever the ConfigMap changes, so Pods roll out automatically.

20
00:02:04,990 --> 00:02:15,288
Hooks run something at a specific point, such as a database migration before an upgrade. Keep migrations backward compatible, and remember that a failing hook fails the release.

21
00:02:15,388 --> 00:02:21,254
Test charts with helm lint, render them and validate with kubeconform, and run helm test.

22
00:02:22,604 --> 00:02:29,814
Kustomize is built into kubectl. You keep plain YAML in a base, and describe only the differences in overlays.

23
00:02:29,914 --> 00:02:39,572
The production overlay sets the namespace and a name prefix, changes the image tag and replica count, generates a ConfigMap, and applies a patch with bigger resources.

24
00:02:39,672 --> 00:02:43,650
Every file stays valid Kubernetes YAML. There's no templating.

25
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,538
kubectl kustomize prints the result, diff shows what would change, and apply dash k applies it.

26
00:02:51,638 --> 00:02:59,136
ConfigMap generators add a content hash to the name and update every reference, so a config change rolls the Pods automatically.

27
00:02:59,236 --> 00:03:05,454
Note that common labels and patches strategic merge are deprecated. Use labels and patches instead.

28
00:03:06,804 --> 00:03:08,238
Which should you choose?

29
00:03:08,338 --> 00:03:13,836
Helm is the standard for installing third-party software, and it has releases and rollback built in.

30
00:03:13,936 --> 00:03:21,034
Kustomize keeps plain YAML that's easy to review. Many teams use Helm for third-party tools, and either one for their own apps.

31
00:03:22,384 --> 00:03:24,170
A few good practices.

32
00:03:24,270 --> 00:03:33,592
Pin chart versions, and keep values files and overlays in Git. Review third-party charts before installing them, and look at the diff before production changes.

33
00:03:33,692 --> 00:03:41,894
Don't edit Helm-managed resources by hand, because the next upgrade overwrites your change. And never put secrets in values files.

34
00:03:43,244 --> 00:03:44,454
Let's recap.

35
00:03:44,554 --> 00:03:51,460
Helm turns a chart plus values into a release with revisions. Template carefully, and check the output with helm template.

36
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:57,522
Kustomize applies overlays to a plain base, and its generators make config changes roll your Pods.

37
00:03:57,622 --> 00:04:01,504
Next, you'll learn a systematic approach to troubleshooting a cluster.
